ploys a multi-dimensional filtration pipeline to target hard samples featuring nuanced input complexity and misleading surface bias, augmenting each with expert-annotated, atomic rubrics derived strictly from instructions. Comprehensive experiments reveal a substantial capability gap between human-annotated and model-generated rubrics, indicating that even state-of-the-art models struggle to autonomously specify valid evaluation criteria, lagging considerably behind human-guided performance."}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "4cab2be72ea772303b1883903780d429f0190e0567181cd8f55d7a8552f68aa7", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "unrecognized_reasoning_limits", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.1109/jiot.2026.3697293", "doi:10.48550/arxiv.2603.01562", "openalex:W7162437951", "openalex:W7133309114", "arxiv:2603.01562"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.1109/jiot.2026.3697293", "https://ueaeprints.uea.ac.uk/id/eprint/104587/1/Multi-AI_Agent.pdf", "https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2603.01562", "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.01562"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}
